{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgKaCa1RpMiaFqzmiJ5iS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The verdict is filed at `docs/reviews/wi-851/003-ADJUDICATE-5a56d69.md`, committed once as 5768ae162 with `WI: WI-851` and `Loop-Session: 20261021T045653Z-213020` in one trailer block.\n\n**Amendment: `VERDICT: MEANING rows=8`.** Every row's obligation changed: the held-rung guard moved from \"which writer made the commit\" to \"what state lands from a lane\", and that touches all eight. I'd bless five and not the other three.\n- **Blessed:**\n  - **SR-208:** widened to all eight snapshotted registries and any lane, with the bound CLARITY exception.\n  - **SR-228:** naming the verdict is now required only when one is supplied, which matches the Done-when.\n  - **LLR-278:** its held-rung clause is retired into the lane rule.\n  - **LLR-246:** rewritten as the lane rule, adding no new code names.\n  - **TC-241:** now covers SR-228 as well.\n- **Not blessed:**\n  - **LLR-249:** it swaps `acceptance_record.` for `held_status.` in the row text. The amendment adds a module name, and the swap was only needed because the function moved modules in this lane, which is the fragility the naming rule guards against.\n  - **TC-243:** it adds `APPROVAL_RUNGS`, `kitlib/authority` and `agent_common` to its Method.\n  - **TC-278:** it adds `amendment_scope` and `reattested_between`. Its Expected also opens with \"Every such id…\", and the sentence that \"such\" refers to wasn't copied in. And it verifies SR-208 and LLR-246 but no longer claims anything about held rungs in Expected.\n\n**First approval: `OUTCOME: APPROVE rows=2`.** LLR-359 is the approved-text digest and the act turn refusing before launch when a row's text moved after its verdict. TC-398 matches it sentence for sentence, and its 14 evidence tests exist and pass, including the malformed-identity refusal at the act turn.\n\n**This blocks part of the act.** The three unblessed rows stay drifted in the design and test-case registries, and the snapshot refuses to copy a registry with a drifted row nobody has re-attested. So the act turn can take the system-requirements registry (SR-208, SR-228) now. LLR-278, LLR-246, TC-241, LLR-359 and TC-398 wait until LLR-249, TC-243 and TC-278 are fixed and re-sit.\n\nThe fix is spine text only, and the verdict's \"Returns answered in the lane\" section drafts it: drop the module qualifier, restate the TC sentences in plain words, give \"such id\" its antecedent, and fix TC-278's held-tier claim. I used that heading rather than `## Dispositions` so intake doesn't mint a row. Its draft title is 137 characters, over the 120-character limit I checked earlier titles against, so it needs shortening if anyone moves it into a real `## Dispositions` block.\n\nChecks: `trace.py --strict` exits 0, all 102 evidence nodes resolve, and the three fast suites pass (152 tests). The commit hooks passed, including `held-status`. They printed seven readability worsenings, none in a gating measure, and again said one declared check did not run, so that check didn't grade this commit."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8077,"cache_read_input_tokens":443364,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8077},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"c2f32322-1f6f-4f08-bc6a-e8dd0d9535aa","timestamp":"2026-10-23T17:36:11.098Z","request_id":"req_011CgKaCZEnz13de6unWXLHv"}
